A||||5||ThatRickGuy||Altanator/diodes?||||1||
Z||000000||ThatRickGuy||12-20-2000||10:34 AM||||Question, on an '88 v-6 are the diodes in the altanator, or is there a seperate control circuit? I asak because my voltage is doing something odd and I think it may be diodes shorting out. Here's the exact description:<P>Voltmeter used to sit a bit high, just short of the begining of the red zone, aprox 15 volts probrably. Recently, I've head a squeeling noise (doesn't sound exactly like a belt, may be the bearings in the alt, or could just be the a funny belt noise) and the voltage will drop down, heater fan will slow, lights dim, etc, but it'll remain stable at either 12 or 9 volts w/ the noise. Put it in nuetral and reved, no change, sometimes turning off/down electrical devices will work, volts will return to their normal place. other times nothing helps. Only happens while at slow (idling) speeds or sitting at stop lights, no problems while driving.<P>So it's either the alt or the diodes, I just wanted to know if they are in the same unit or if this is a 2 piece opperation.<P>Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||12-20-2000||11:00 AM||||The diodes are inside the alternator.<P>call around the service centers. most of the chains will test it free. some of the smaller ones will to.||||reg||1
Z||000002||hugh||12-20-2000||05:37 PM||||It sounds as if you need to tighten the belt or replace it if it is already tight.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mark||12-20-2000||06:22 PM||||Rick,<P>I doubt the problem is with the diodes. When they fail, they fail. Bad field diodes usually result in no alternator output.  Bad stator diodes will usually kill your battery. <P>Since your alternator seems to work OK at high speeds, the diodes are probably OK.  It could be a regulator problem (the regulator is also built into the alt), or possibly a bad ground connection between engine and chassis.  Make sure the ground braid which runs from the AC bracket to the decklid hinge bolt is tight and clean.  You can check it by measuring the DC voltage drop between the engine block and the chassis (should be very low, like a few tenths of a volt).  If the grounds all check out, and you're SURE you don't have a loose or bad belt, then have the alt checked at one of the chain parts stores like Pep Boys, Autozone, etc.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||My7Fieros||12-20-2000||10:15 PM||||Thats a great question Rick. Ive got the same prob on my 88 V6. Ive been told (as I assumed), that the alt. was simply starting to "head south". I did, however, put some belt grip on it..the kind in the tube, NOT that worthless crap in a spray can, and it did miracles...also something else Ive noticed..when I put new alt belts on my 88 Formula and 86 Gt at the same time, the belt appeared to be almost an inch longer than the ones that came off the cars.<BR>Several people here on the forum advised that this was a common prob w/certain brands of belts...kind've a "universal" belt...(its a Kelly brand).<BR>Someone on here advised me to get a Dayco brand belt..said it fit MUCH better...Im gonna do that when I replace the altinator on the Forumla.....hopefully it'll work.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Bob Englert||12-20-2000||10:35 PM||||When I had my Formula, the 'correct' belt from Advance Auto was too long, even with the alternator at the end of the tension adjustment slot.  I went back to the parts store and asked for the next shorter sized belt of the same type, it was about 1 or 1 1/2 inches shorter.  Was a little harder to get on but fit very well and was comfortably in the adjustment range of the alternator bracket.<P>You might want to try this if the belt seems too long or stretches past usable travel limits.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
